# Week 8 — Quality Requirements and Prioritisation


## 1. Quality Requirements
This week, I reviewed the quality requirements for the Community Volunteer & Event Coordination System
NFR01 — Usability (proposed): At least 4 out of 5 first-time volunteers should be able to find an event and complete an application without assistance during a usability test.
NFR02 — Performance (proposed): Under a load of 25 concurrent users, at least 95% of event searches should display results within 3 seconds
These targets are proposals and require confirmation from stakeholders


## 2. MoSCoW Prioritisation
The following priorities are provisional:

Must:FR01 Account Registration, FR02 Volunteer Profile Management, FR04 Event and Role Management, FR05 Qualification Verification.
Should:FR03 Upload Qualification Evidence, FR06 Event Change Notifications, FR07 Roster Management.
Could: Waitlist and calendar view
Won't: No requirements have been placed in this category because no release exclusions have been confirmed
FR08 — Submit Application — remains a candidate requirement pending confirmation


## 3. Prototype Updates
I reviewed and updated all five Week 7 prototype screens:
Browse Events
Role Details and Requirements
Application Form
Validation Error
Application Submitted
The updated screens help demonstrate how volunteers interact with the system and how the requirements could be implemented


## 4. Open Questions
What response time do stakeholders expect when searching for events
How should usability be measured and approved
Who verifies volunteer qualifications?
What should happen when Garda vetting is pending or expired

## 5. Reflection
This week's work helped me understand how to make quality requirements measurable and how to prioritise functional requirements using MoSCoW. The proposed targets and priorities still need stakeholder validation.
